      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: keep mapped hook content provenance scoped [AI]",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where operators using multiple hook mappings could have a non-Gmail hook treated as Gmail-sourced when the request body included a matching `source` value. That could make external hook content skip the wrapping behavior the operator expected for the actual mapping.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nMapped hook provenance now comes from the matched hook path and resolved hook session key instead of a caller-controlled body field. The Gmail path and Gmail-scoped sessions still keep Gmail provenance; unrelated mappings stay classified as webhook content unless their own mapping explicitly opts into unsafe external content.\n\nThis PR was prepared with AI assistance.\n\n## User Impact\n\nOperators can keep Gmail-specific unsafe external content settings enabled without that setting spilling over to unrelated hook mappings. Existing Gmail hooks and explicit mapping-level unsafe-content configuration continue to work as before.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- AWS Crabbox focused validation on head `74b5c41b3b8b2a442251efca71a3f68496f4d8dd`: `corepack pnpm test src/gateway/server.hooks.test.ts`\n- Result: `run_cdb8a2c517d8`, provider `aws`, lease `cbx_09d75e0f940a`, 50 hook tests passed across the gateway projects.\n- Added regression coverage for `/hooks/github` receiving `source: \"gmail\"` while dispatching with `externalContentSource: \"webhook\"` and no unsafe-content override.\n",
